  "preamble": "The transformation of a voiced aspirated stop into a voiceless fricative is a core component of Grimm's Law for PIE *bh. In Latin, PIE word-initial *bʰ became f (frāter, fīdō); several Austronesian languages (Tetum, Thao, Tsou) independently changed *b to f.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Spirantization, Lenition, Devoicing",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Latin",
      "languageFamily": "Italic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*bhre-",
          "to": "frater",
          "note": "PIE voiced aspirate *bh shifted to f in word-initial position."
        },
        {
          "from": "*bheidh-",
          "to": "fido",
          "note": "PIE *bh shifted to f in word-initial position."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Tetum",
      "languageFamily": "Central Malayo-Polynesian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *bulan 'moon, month'",
          "to": "fulan",
          "note": "PMP *b became f in Tetum, alongside the shift of *p to h; a few words such as betun (< *bətuŋ) and belar (< *bəlaj) keep b as exceptions."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *bulu 'body hair'",
          "to": "fulun"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *bunuq 'to kill'",
          "to": "funu"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *bəRas 'rice'",
          "to": "fos"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Thao",
      "languageFamily": "Western Plains",
      "examples": [
